{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792365850,"nonce":"jXdQ1xnXwWnyO2BegsOnX1hTpxU=","message":"**Done in b973ba1**\n\nThe window is two integer signals, set by watches that react only when a boundary moves, so a scroll inside the built window runs nothing. The row elements are pooled: row r of the document is shown by slot r mod capacity (capacity is the view's rows plus nine), each slot's line and gutter elements are bound to a row signal, and a slot keeps its token elements per kind, so a row taking a slot changes text and position and never a token's kind (a changed data-token restyled the element and was nearly half of the step time). The fold button of a slot is always there and answers only while its row opens a fold.\n\nMeasured on a 3200-line source in a 420 px editor, flush and layout per scroll step, Ash interpreter (relative numbers only): 11 px steps 44.8 ms mean before, 9.4 after; 52 px steps 80.3 before, 33.1 after; 5 px steps 40.0 before, 6.3 after. The DOM has 30 line rows and 422 elements; scrolling builds none once every slot has shown a row.\n\nKnown differences: the DOM order of the rows is by slot, not by document line, and slots with no row (a short document) stay as empty hidden elements. CodeComponents has the checks: a scroll inside the window leaves the built rows as they were, scrolling twelve lines creates no element, and each gutter number stays level with its row.\n\nRayDE's scripts/scroll-bench.sh is the measurement that matters; its numbers are still to come.","files":null}]}